          "snippet": "single molecule ͉ subnanometer resolution ͉ signal-to-noise ratio S ince the discovery that optical gradients could stably trap micrometer-sized dielectric particles (1), gradient optical traps, or optical tweezers, have been used in a large variety of applications ranging from microfabrication (2,3) to the study of colloidal hydrodynamics (4-6) and nonequilibrium thermodynamics (7)(8)(9)(10). In particular, the use of calibrated optical springs has provided unprecedented new insight on the mechanical properties of single biological molecules and the molecular motors that manipulate them in the cell (11)(12)(13)(14).",
